The board state has 19 pieces (10 x and 9 o), confirming it is o's turn to move. No immediate winning move exists for o (exhaustive check of all 7 legal drops shows that none produces four-in-a-row). Likewise x has no immediate win.

However, x possesses a ready-made diagonal of three (positions (5,1), (4,2), (3,3)) whose completion square is (2,4). In addition the two empty squares (1,0) and (1,1) form a reciprocal pair: each becomes a winning square once the other is occupied by x, because they complete a horizontal four on row 1. Consequently any move by o that leaves both of those squares free allows the following forced sequence:

- x occupies one square of the pair, creating an immediate threat;
- o is forced to take the other square;
- x then begins filling column 4;
- when x later plays (3,4) the square (2,4) becomes live and o must block it;
- that block automatically exposes (1,4), which is now also a winning square for x (the three on row 1 having already been established);
- x takes (1,4) and wins.

The only move that breaks the sequence is the occupation of (1,1). After o places at (1,1) the reciprocal pair is destroyed, the horizontal three-in-a-row on row 1 can no longer be formed with a single stone, and (1,4) ceases to be a winning square. Column 4's latent threat at (2,4) remains, but it is now an isolated single threat that can be answered when it becomes live; no forced win for x follows.

All other columns leave the fatal pair intact and therefore lose against perfect reply. Hence the unique optimal action is to play column 1.

Final Answer: 1

